## 2026-10-13 15:58 IST — OE Package 2 correction: React commit safety and mutation-check lifecycle

Reason: Sol's review of Package 2 left two blockers. (A) `useOperationsCapability` advanced its
entry-generation ref and retired/aborted pending checks *during render*, so a concurrent React render
that was suspended, interrupted or discarded could abort the still-committed entry's live verification
and destroy its admission. (B) A mutation pre-flight check whose initiating operation was cancelled
could keep its `pendingChecksRef` gate entry indefinitely when its aborted promise never settled,
failing the dispatch gate closed and blocking a newer, authorized Claim or Release. Both are corrected
inside the one existing shared authority: no new authorization architecture, no OE Workspace redesign,
and the Package 2 same-account entry behaviour is preserved.

Correction A (render is side-effect free; ownership advances only at commit). The render-phase `entryRef`
mutation and the render-phase `retireObsoleteEntryChecks(...)` call are gone. The hook now holds the
*committed* entry as a ref (synchronous source of truth for continuations) plus a state mirror (drives
the render gate and the verification effect). Render only *derives* whether the requested surface is the
committed one: `entryAdmitted` requires `committedEntry.key === workspaceEntry` and
`admission.generation === committedEntry.generation`, so the first render of a requested entry is
withheld with no effect having to run. A `useLayoutEffect` — which React runs only for a render it
actually committed — advances the generation, retires the superseded entry's obsolete checks (sync gate
removal + transport abort) and publishes the new committed entry; it early-returns when the committed
key already matches, so a StrictMode remount neither re-advances ownership nor retires the new entry's
own checks. The entry/background verification is initiated from a passive effect keyed on the committed
entry and is deduped by the existing single-in-flight sharing, so StrictMode's double-invoked effects
re-establish verification instead of leaving the entry unverified. `applyGrant`, `isDispatchAuthorized`
and the default `entryGeneration` all read the committed ref, so a grant earned by an earlier entry can
never admit a later one, and rapid A → B → A cannot reuse an obsolete admission generation.

Correction B (a cancelled mutation check stops gating immediately). Each pending check now carries a
`detach()` that removes the initiating operation's abort listener. The check's `forwardAbort` listener
runs `detach()`, `retireOwnPendingCheck()` and only then `control.abort()`. `retireOwnPendingCheck`
removes *only its own* id (and only while the registered controller is still its own), synchronously and
without awaiting network settlement, so a cancelled mutation whose transport never settles stops failing
`isDispatchAuthorized` in the same tick and cannot block a newer, valid operation. An already-aborted
caller signal returns before any gate is registered; `finally` and both retirement helpers call
`detach()` so listeners cannot accumulate; and a late result from a cancelled check is still refused by
`isCurrent()` / `control.signal.aborted` before any verdict is applied, so it can neither restore
authority nor dispatch. A genuinely current, still-pending check continues to fail the gate closed, and
the pre-mutation authorization requirement is untouched.

