Architecture and design
- The registry keeps its operation/session/credential/authority ownership and adds the display
  dimension a completion needs, in the same shape the refusal side already used:
  `displayContextGeneration` (the display context captured before the first await),
  `acceptedDisplayContext` (a display change the operation published *itself* as an authorised step of
  its own flow), `markAcceptedDisplayContext(operation)` (live operation only, re-recorded per
  publication) and the pure, lock-independent `displayContextIsOwned(operation)` verdict. The verdict
  is deliberately readable after `finish` has released the mutation lock, because a caller reporting a
  confirmed command must ask it in the same tick as its own write; the commit guard is never used
  there.
- A. Authoritative business completion and B. Local UI publication are separated everywhere. A
  validated success keeps its status, its `applied` result and its truthful confirmation; only its
  *local publication* (installing the returned root, navigating, dropping detail, writing a drawer
  outcome, or a record-named notice) is refused once the display context is no longer the command's.
  No suppressed publication becomes an uncertain outcome, and no confirmed command is retransmitted.
- One shared verdict, not per-command patches: `applyAuthoritativeSession`/`applyAuthoritativeRequest`
  take the operation and refuse in the same tick as their write; `openDetail` includes the display
  verdict in the context it validates and records the operation's own open; every caller re-takes the
  verdict (`commandContextHolds`) after the awaited reconciliation for its record-named notice; the
  stopped-command notice (`missingVersionNotice`) is bound the same way.
- The scheduling drawer is bound to its *instance*, not its Session id: a `scheduleDrawerRef`
  `{ sessionId, generation }` is captured before the booking starts and moved on every open and close
  (leaving the detail, withdrawing a denied root and an account change all close it through the same
  helper), and `scheduleOutcomeContextHolds(operation, drawer)` requires both the display context and
  the exact drawer instance before any drawer outcome is written.
- Legitimate operation-owned transitions are preserved: a coordination that replaces its initiating
  Request with the returned Session (or a claim that opens the root it claimed, or a release that drops
  the root it released) records its own display change, so the rest of that flow — including the
  "work lists could not be refreshed" notice after a failed follow-up refresh — still publishes. Any
  later user navigation or remount moves the generation past it and invalidates everything further.

